      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine how many books are left over by repacking the books from the original boxes of 39 books each into new boxes of 40 books each.\n\n**Step 1: Calculation of total books**\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of books:\n\\[\n1335 \\text{ boxes} \\times 39 \\text{ books per box} = 52215 \\text{ books}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Repack the books**\n\nNext, we want to repack these books into new boxes of 40 books each:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{52215}{40} \\right\\rfloor \\text{ boxes} \\times 40 \\text{ books per box} \\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor 1305.375 \\right\\rfloor = 1305 \\text{ boxes}\n\\]\n\nThis means that the total number of books in new boxes of 40 books each is:\n\\[\n1305 \\times 40 = 52200 \\text{ books}\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Calculate leftover books**\n\nTo find the leftover books, we subtract the number of books that fit into the new boxes from the total number of books:\n\\[\n52215 \\text{ books} - 52200 \\text{ books} = 15 \\text{ books}\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nHence, after packing as many boxes of 40 books each as possible, Melvin has \\(\\boxed{15}\\) books left over.",
