      "text": "To determine how many books left over, we first calculate the total number of books in the warehouse:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total books} = 1335 \\times 39\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property, we break down the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n1335 \\times 39 = 1335 \\times (40 - 1) = 1335 \\times 40 - 1335\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(1335 \\times 40\\) and perform the subtraction:\n\n\\[\n1335 \\times 40 = 1335 \\times (4 \\times 10) = (1335 \\times 4) \\times 10\n\\]\n\n\\[\n1335 \\times 4 = 5340\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n1335 \\times 40 = 5340 \\times 10 = 53400\n\\]\n\nSubtract the original number of books from this result:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Leftover books} = 53400 - 1335 = 52065\n\\]\n\nTo confirm, check the number of full boxes shaped as \\(40 \\times 40\\):\n\n\\[\n40^2 = 1600\n\\]\n\nThe number of boxes filled to \\(40\\) books is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of boxes shaped as } 40 \\times 40 = \\frac{53400}{1600} = 33 \\text{ full boxes}\n\\]\n\nThe leftover books:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Leftover books} = 53400 - 33 \\times 1600 = 53400 - 52800 = 600\n\\]\n\nWe verified that:\n\n\\[\n53400 - 1335 = 52065\n\\]\n\nThus, Melvin ends up with \\(\\boxed{600}\\) leftover books.",
