      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine how many books Melvin will have left over after repacking them into boxes with 40 books each, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of books:**\n   Since there are 1335 boxes and each box contains 39 books:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of books} = 1335 \\times 39\n   \\]\n\n2. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   Using the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 39 = 1335 \\times (40 - 1) = 1335 \\times 40 - 1335 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 40 = 53400\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 1 = 1335\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   53400 - 1335 = 52065\n   \\]\n   So, the total number of books is 52065.\n\n3. **Determine the number of full boxes of 40 books each that can be packed:**\n   Divide the total number of books by 40:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of full boxes} = \\frac{52065}{40}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Perform the division:**\n   Using long division or a calculator, we find:\n   \\[\n   52065 \\div 40 = 1301.625\n   \\]\n   Since we can only have whole boxes, we take the integer part of the division, which is 1301. This means Melvin can pack 1301 boxes of 40 books each.\n\n5. **Calculate the total number of books in these full boxes:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Books in full boxes} = 1301 \\times 40\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1301 \\times 40 = 52040\n   \\]\n\n6. **Determine the number of remaining books:**\n   Subtract the number of books in the full boxes from the total number of books:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Remaining books} = 52065 - 52040 = 25\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, after packing as many boxes as possible, Melvin has \\boxed{25} books left over.",
